WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.151
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.151 --> 00:00:07.150
PAUL NEWSON: I'll introduce
myself a little bit.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:10.060
As the slide says, my
name's Paul Newson.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.580
I go by the title SRE Advocate.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.010
I feel the need to explain
that because to my knowledge,

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.843
I'm the first person
to ever call themselves

00:00:16.843 --> 00:00:18.910
an SRE Advocate.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.334
We have this thing at Google
called the developer advocate

00:00:21.334 --> 00:00:22.750
which is somebody,
an engineer who

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.750
works in developer relations
who goes out and talks

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:31.430
to developers and interfaces
with the developer community.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.580
I like to do that
same thing but I

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.770
like to do it with
SRE and DevOps folks.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.480
And I recently did a
mission control rotation.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.100
That's a thing at
Google where you

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.140
get to embed yourself
with an SRE team

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.060
and walk in their shoes
and carry the pager

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:50.050
and live an SRE's life for
between 6 and 12 months.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.570
It was a fantastic experience.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.690
I was really fortunate to
have the chance to do it.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.754
And now I'm here to
hopefully share with you

00:00:57.754 --> 00:00:59.170
some of the things
I learned along

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:03.040
the way about designing
reliable systems with Cloud

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.180
infrastructure.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:07.820
So if we're going to talk about
designing reliable systems,

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:12.690
I do want to get really precise
about what we mean by reliable.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.787
So when you look up the word
reliable, what you get is this.

00:01:15.787 --> 00:01:17.120
There's some good words in here.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.310
Consistently good in
quality or performance,

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.650
sounds kind of like
what we're going for.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:24.230
But I actually think the
more insightful phrase here

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:26.390
is able to be trusted.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:29.960
Because what it comes down
to, if your users trust

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.030
your system, then your
system is reliable.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.240
If you have earned that trust,
then your system is reliable.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.874
If your users don't
trust your service,

00:01:37.874 --> 00:01:39.290
then your service
is not reliable.

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.880
And that's going to be different
depending on your service.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.220
So let's take this
system for example.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.090
The reliability required
for this particular system

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.830
for those users looking
at those windows

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.470
at the top of the space
shuttle is very, very high

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:53.270
for them to trust the system.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.140
Compare that with, for example,
an entertainment service.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.050
Users still want to
trust this system

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.120
and reliability is
still very important,

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:05.330
but I think it's pretty clear
that the level of reliability

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.430
required by a life
safety critical system

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:10.250
and the level of reliability
required by an entertainment

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:13.850
system to earn the users'
trust is different.

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:15.410
And that's because
the consequence

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:19.394
of a brief temporary outage of
the system is very different.

00:02:19.394 --> 00:02:20.810
In one case, you
don't get to play

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.310
your game for a few minutes.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:26.380
In another case, it may
endanger somebody's life.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.530
So, we've talked
a bit about trust.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.100
Your goal is to get your
users to trust your service.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.220
Trust is context dependent.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.770
It's different for life
safety versus entertainment

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.990
versus financial versus
retail versus whatever

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.150
it is your systems do.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:44.310
And the last point I
want to make about trust

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:45.940
is that it's really
hard to measure.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.000
In fact, I don't know
how to measure trust.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.520
Maybe there's a psychologist
in the audience today

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.860
who knows how to measure
trust and can approach me

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.787
after the session and
tell me all about it

00:02:54.787 --> 00:02:57.120
and that would be a great
conversation I'd love to have.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.520
But the engineer currently
standing on the stage

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.680
doesn't know how
to measure trust.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.660
Moreover, I have no idea
how you would measure

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.830
trust in a real time way to see
if your service is currently

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.290
delivering on that
goal of user trust.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.520
So we need to find a proxy.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.895
We need to find
something we know

00:03:12.895 --> 00:03:16.770
how to measure which is a
proxy for that user's trust.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.710
And this brings us to the
concept of a service level

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.720
indicator.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.020
The SRE book says, a carefully
defined quantitative measure

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:27.670
of some aspect of the level
of service that is provided.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.090
So basically, it's something you
can measure about your service.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.750
In this particular example,
I've got a graph here.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.750
It's showing an
error percentage.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:41.850
So this particular service
that I pulled this graph from

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:45.000
is a fairly straightforward
HTTP request response

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.260
kind of service.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:52.230
And this graph is showing us
the percentage of requests which

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:55.590
resulted in a 500 class error.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:59.100
It is very unlikely you can
read the scale on the left,

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.200
but it's not that important.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.560
The important thing is
you look at these spikes

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.570
on the left of the graph
and you're like wow,

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.100
those look pretty scary.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.090
Are they a problem?

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:11.660
Maybe, maybe not.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.480
It really depends
on your service.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.420
So what transforms a
service level indicator

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:20.200
into a service level
objective is adding a goal.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.510
Now this is where we have
to look at the things

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:25.570
we know how to measure
and use the insight

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:27.790
that we have about
the services we offer

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:31.030
to decide what is
a threshold that

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:34.330
makes sense for this
service level indicator.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:36.430
So again, let's go
back to this example

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:40.630
where we have this HTTP
request response pattern.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.990
And in this particular
case, let's say

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:47.350
this service is something where
every HTTP request can easily

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.770
be retried if it fails.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:56.220
In that case three nines might
be perfectly adequate to--

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:01.120
a perfectly adequate threshold
to set as your service level

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.590
objective.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:06.280
Because if someone gets
a 500 class response,

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:10.270
they can retry the request
and they'll get a success.

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.220
And the client will
probably do it for them

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.740
and the user will never
know that a 500 was served.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:18.700
In other words, below
99.9% success rate,

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.440
user trust is not eroded.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:21.937
Now this is not a
universal thing.

00:05:21.937 --> 00:05:23.020
It depends on the service.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.390
And this is where we
get to apply our user

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:27.520
empathy and our
engineering judgment

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.440
to choosing these objectives.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:32.874
So in this case--

00:05:32.874 --> 00:05:34.790
I know it's very difficult
to read this slide,

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:39.080
but those spikes don't
even come close to 0.1%.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.400
In fact, they're one order
of magnitude below that.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.490
So these spikes in this case,
as scary as they may look,

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.844
are no problem at all.

00:05:46.844 --> 00:05:49.260
The last point I'd like to
make before moving on from this

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.930
is to talk about service
level agreements.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.960
A lot of people use
the term service

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:56.490
level objective
and service level

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.127
agreement interchangeably.

00:05:58.127 --> 00:06:00.210
And I think it's very
important to tease these two

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:01.740
things apart.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.620
A service level agreement is
a contract with your users.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:07.920
It is a legally binding contract
between a service provider

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.150
and a customer.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:16.830
Quite often an SLA will contain
an SLO embedded inside it.

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:18.450
But usually, that SLO--

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:20.710
which some might refer
to as an external SLO,

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:23.980
the SLO you're offering
to your customers--

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.709
is different from
the internal solo

00:06:26.709 --> 00:06:28.500
SLO you actually use
to manage your system.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.770
And generally, the internal SLO
will be stricter than the one

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:37.140
that you put in your service
level agreement or SLA.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:40.830
So just to recap, an SLI
is something you measure.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.890
An SLO is an SLI plus a goal.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:45.600
And an SLA or service
level agreement

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.410
is an SLO or service level
objective with penalties.

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:54.080
So I could do a
whole talk on just

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.590
how to do this really well and
give lots and lots of examples

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.740
but that'll have to
wait for another day.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:04.366
I'd like to move on to the
concept of an error budget.

00:07:04.366 --> 00:07:09.800
The error budget is related
to your service level

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:11.360
objective or SLO.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:15.500
If you take, say, a three nines
SLO and then ask the question,

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.370
well how often can I be down,
that gives you a 0.1% error

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:20.960
budget.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.430
It's really the same thing.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:25.310
It's just that sometimes
your error budget

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:27.020
is easier to reason about.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:31.820
For example, if you're
running a three nines service,

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:34.640
that means you get
43 minutes a month

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:38.446
where you get to be down before
you have violated your SLO.

00:07:38.446 --> 00:07:40.070
If you're running a
four nines service,

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:42.170
that drops down to
4.3 minutes a month.

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:44.660
So you can see how this
sort of makes it real

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:48.890
when you think about how
long of a global total outage

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:52.700
can I have before I
have violated my SLO?

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:55.130
And if you look in the
five nines column here,

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:57.410
you can see it's pretty tough.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.590
You get 25.9 seconds
a month before you

00:08:00.590 --> 00:08:01.820
have violated your SLO.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:03.500
That's a pretty high bar.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:07.460
Quite often as you move
to more nines in your SLO,

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:09.920
you also increase
the time window

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:11.500
over which you measure that.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:14.510
So you might measure it over
90 days or a whole year,

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:15.980
for example.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:19.100
So if we look at five
nines over a year,

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:22.400
you're looking at
about five minutes

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:29.030
of a total global outage before
you have violated your SLO.

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:32.690
So, that's not a
whole lot of time.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:35.330
That sort of sets
the bar pretty high.

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:36.799
How do you go about that?

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:39.320
Let's say, for example, that
you have an on call rotation

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:41.030
and your agreement
with your on callers

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:43.669
is that they should be hands
on keyboard troubleshooting

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:47.000
the problem five minutes
after the pager goes off.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:51.440
You've just used up your entire
SLO budget, your entire error

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.369
budget for the year
waiting for your on caller

00:08:54.369 --> 00:08:56.660
to put his hands on-- his or
her hands on the keyboard.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:03.410
In this case, type faster is
probably not the right answer

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:06.500
for how to stay within
your error budget.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:10.790
Instead what you need to do is
apply lots of best practices

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:13.730
to your service to keep the
humans ahead of the service.

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:15.450
To make total global
outages, which

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:17.450
will consume all of your
error budget incredibly

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:21.710
quickly, very, very,
very, very, very rare.

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:25.130
The book is a great start,
I'll plug the book a few times.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:27.650
I can't say enough
good things about it.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:29.120
You have no excuse
not to read it.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:31.500
If you go to this URL,
you can read it for free.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.550
It's out there under a
Creative Commons license.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:39.020
There will be a book signing up
in the fifth nine lounge area.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.390
I don't know when.

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:44.870
But if you go up to the fifth
nine, it should be posted.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:47.300
And you get a chance to meet
some of the authors as well.

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:49.790
And I think they're
giving away maybe 100

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:51.810
copies to the first
100 people up there.

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:55.760
So go check out the fifth nine.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.910
So, how does an error
budget help you?

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:02.930
Well, it helps guide
decision making process

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.800
about a lot of different things.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:07.980
Any time you change
your service,

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:09.960
there's a certain
amount of risk involved

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.370
in that and a certain amount of
effort in making that change.

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:18.252
So for example, should we
deploy this new feature?

00:10:18.252 --> 00:10:20.210
Well there's risk in
deploying the new feature.

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:21.640
There's effort in
deploying the new feature.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:23.440
There is clearly effort
in making the new feature.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:24.460
Should we deploy it now?

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:27.910
Well, look at your SLO,
look at your error budget

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:31.000
have you consumed all of
your budget for this month?

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.150
If the answer is yes,
you should probably wait.

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:36.590
Give your users a break,
deploy that next month.

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:43.312
How should we trade off resource
usage versus reliability?

00:10:43.312 --> 00:10:45.770
For example, and we'll see some
examples later in the talk,

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:47.228
you can over
provision your service

00:10:47.228 --> 00:10:50.570
to make it more reliable
but that adds to your cost.

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:54.770
Is that a worthwhile investment
or can you run a little hotter?

00:10:54.770 --> 00:10:57.084
The answer to that will
be in your error budgets.

00:10:57.084 --> 00:10:59.000
If you are consuming all
of your error budget,

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:00.750
you probably don't
want to run any hotter.

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:03.170
If you're not consuming
all of your error budget,

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:04.760
maybe you should
consider running

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:08.150
your service a little hotter.

00:11:08.150 --> 00:11:10.580
We've got developers who
can write awesome things.

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:12.740
Should they be writing
awesome new features

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:16.850
or should they be
writing code which makes

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:18.149
the service more reliable?

00:11:18.149 --> 00:11:19.940
How do you choose
between these two things?

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:20.731
They're both great.

00:11:20.731 --> 00:11:22.999
Well again, look at
your error budgets.

00:11:22.999 --> 00:11:25.290
Are you consuming all of your
error budget every month,

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:26.109
month after month?

00:11:26.109 --> 00:11:28.400
Well you should probably be
investing a little bit more

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:31.730
in reliability to keep ahead
of the growth of your service.

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:34.790
If you're well within your error
budget, bring on the features

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:39.095
because you're meeting
your SLO easily.

00:11:39.095 --> 00:11:40.970
Here's a good one, should
we do more testing?

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:41.700
Testing's good.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.310
Testing is always good, right?

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:44.990
Again, look at
your error budgets

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:47.100
month after month after month.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:51.740
If you are consistently
putting out releases and not

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:53.420
causing outages
and not consuming

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:56.090
your error budgets, whatever
testing you're doing

00:11:56.090 --> 00:11:57.110
is good enough.

00:11:57.110 --> 00:12:03.170
And testing more is only going
to add cost that provides

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:05.030
no value to your users.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.190
On the other hand,
if your releases

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.950
are not super reliable,
if they cause outages,

00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:11.510
if they're not
going out reliably

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:13.792
and are quite often rolled
back, maybe investing

00:12:13.792 --> 00:12:15.500
in testing infrastructure
is exactly what

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:17.490
you should be doing.

00:12:17.490 --> 00:12:20.510
But I'm not here to talk about
any of those things today.

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:23.100
I'm here to talk
about architecture.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:25.790
How do you architect
your system to minimize

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:27.830
total global outages?

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:33.044
And how do you trade off
the cost of doing so?

00:12:33.044 --> 00:12:34.460
How do you know
whether you should

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:36.050
invest in the cost of doing so?

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:38.580
And of course it comes back to
your service level objective.

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:41.510
How can I design my service
to meet its service level

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:44.720
objectives with minimum cost?

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:47.210
So the particular type of--

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:48.780
the particular aspect
of architecture

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:50.488
I'm going to spend a
lot of time on today

00:12:50.488 --> 00:12:55.220
is how do you use our
instances, zones, regions,

00:12:55.220 --> 00:13:00.125
and multi-regions to
assemble a resilient system?

00:13:02.634 --> 00:13:04.800
And this is going to be the
focus of the talk today.

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:07.490
So first thing up is instances.

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:10.220
This is our most
granular building block

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:12.020
on the Google Cloud platform.

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:14.990
This is a virtual
machine running

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:17.210
in one of our data centers.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:20.870
Not surprisingly, it runs
on a physical machine.

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:23.690
Also not surprisingly,
there are bad things

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:25.190
that can happen to
physical machines

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:27.314
and you're probably all
familiar with these things.

00:13:27.314 --> 00:13:28.160
DRAM can go bad.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.090
NICs can go bad.

00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:30.740
Power can fail.

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:35.840
You can have operating system
bugs which crash the machine.

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:38.780
If the physical instance
dies unexpectedly,

00:13:38.780 --> 00:13:44.210
then your virtual
instance goes with it.

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:46.544
Probably no big surprise there.

00:13:46.544 --> 00:13:47.960
There are also bad
things that can

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:50.360
happen to the rack as a whole.

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:51.830
The power to the rack can fail.

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:54.200
The networking can
fail at the rack level.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.440
Again, any of these things
happen, your instance

00:13:57.440 --> 00:13:58.860
is going to go with it.

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:02.170
Again, probably no
big surprise there.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:06.050
Now, if you're managing 10
machines or 100 machines

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:08.120
these kind of failures
are actually quite rare.

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:11.450
But when your scale goes up to
thousands or tens of thousands

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:14.810
of machines, the
rare becomes common.

00:14:14.810 --> 00:14:19.480
And Google and
other companies that

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:24.290
have been running large
fleets of machines

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:28.850
have learned that handling
reliability and software

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:32.270
is more flexible and more cost
effective than handling it

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:32.810
in hardware.

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:35.090
So the reason I'm
not up here talking

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:38.930
about how we have the world's
most reliable hardware

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:40.790
is because these
things are generally

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:43.100
handled at the software layer.

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:46.190
That has advantages in that
software is much easier

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:47.720
to change and
improve on over time,

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:50.750
but it also allows you to
choose different service level

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:52.610
objectives for different
services running

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:55.084
on the same hardware platforms.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:01.240
But let's get back
to your instance.

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:02.530
Something bad happened.

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:05.200
The physical machine
failed unexpectedly.

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:06.460
What happens to your instance?

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:09.400
Well, we start it on some
other physical machine

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:13.720
automatically for you,
unless you've told us not to.

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:15.610
But wait, you might be asking.

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:18.190
What about the stuff that
was on my hard drives?

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:22.030
Well if you're storing your
stuff in persistent disks,

00:15:22.030 --> 00:15:24.790
though that data is already
stored in a distributed

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:27.490
fashion throughout the
cluster, which means

00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:29.260
we can still mount
your drives even

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:31.468
though we're running on a
different physical machine.

00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:36.230
The exception to
this is local SSD

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:38.539
which as the name
implies is in fact

00:15:38.539 --> 00:15:40.580
local to the physical
machine you are running on.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:45.110
So in a scenario like this,
those disks are ephemeral.

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:49.370
Now, you need to be aware
of that so you choose--

00:15:49.370 --> 00:15:50.780
you use them appropriately.

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:53.030
Some great uses I've
seen for these things

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:56.280
are as caches because if the
cache goes away, well great,

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:58.550
you'll just refill
it somewhere else.

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:00.050
I've seen it use
really effectively

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:02.360
for other sort of
cache like things

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:05.690
like shuffle space for large
high performance MapReduce

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:06.890
jobs.

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:09.080
And I've also heard of
people running open source

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:13.610
databases which can span
across multiple machines

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:16.310
and doing that effectively
with local SSD.

00:16:18.774 --> 00:16:20.190
So let's talk about
the difference

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:24.840
between planned and
unplanned failures.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:27.810
Occasionally we need to do stuff
to the machines in our data

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:30.390
centers or to the racks
in our data centers.

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:31.950
This would be
planned maintenance.

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:33.324
This is different
than what we've

00:16:33.324 --> 00:16:35.170
been talking about before.

00:16:35.170 --> 00:16:37.440
The big difference here
is we know it's coming

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:39.120
and we can use
our live migration

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:43.020
feature to get your instance
out of the way of the planned

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:43.520
maintenance.

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:46.230
So basically, this stuff
is invisible to you.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:48.540
And that's true
even for local SSD.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:51.480
We're transferring the
contents of the RAM

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:53.190
of your virtual machine
from one instance

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:56.190
to-- from one physical
machine to another already,

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:58.830
we can do the same for the
bytes on the local SSD.

00:17:01.421 --> 00:17:02.920
Now I've spent a
lot of time talking

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:06.324
about how instances fail and I
don't want this to scare you.

00:17:06.324 --> 00:17:07.490
I mean, it's a fact of life.

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:11.030
We all have run hardware, we all
have had computers fail on us.

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:15.010
The computers in Google's
data center are no different.

00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:21.430
But we understand that if
your experience on our Cloud

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:23.500
platform was that
my instances are

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:25.420
having unplanned
failures all the time,

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:27.770
we would lose your trust.

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:31.420
So not surprisingly,
we have an internal SLO

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:34.330
around how much of that is
acceptable before we need

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:36.890
to do things to mitigate it.

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:39.610
And what that means
is in aggregate

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:43.190
over lots of instances,
most instances

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:45.880
will have the opportunity
to live a long, healthy life

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:48.040
if that's what they want to do.

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:50.530
That doesn't mean any
individual instance can't

00:17:50.530 --> 00:17:52.150
suffer an unplanned outage.

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:54.010
But over a large
number of instances,

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:57.430
you'll probably find it does
not happen often enough to erode

00:17:57.430 --> 00:17:59.200
your trust of the service.

00:17:59.200 --> 00:18:03.130
And that's us choosing an SLO
with user empathy for you.

00:18:06.591 --> 00:18:08.090
So now we've talked
about instances.

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:12.890
Let's step one
level up to zones.

00:18:12.890 --> 00:18:14.660
Instances live in zones.

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:18.300
Zones roughly
correspond to a cluster.

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:21.350
Now the word cluster
is a very vague term

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:22.850
and has different
meanings depending

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:24.410
on who you're talking to.

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:27.380
In this context, a
cluster is basically

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:30.770
an extremely well
connected set of computers.

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:34.580
If you've read the paper we
wrote about our Jupiter cluster

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:36.710
level networking, that's
what we're talking about.

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:40.370
You're all in one big
happy Jupiter family

00:18:40.370 --> 00:18:43.100
and you can talk very low
latency, very high bandwidth

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:45.120
to each other.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:47.750
So if your SLO
requires more then

00:18:47.750 --> 00:18:50.700
you can expect from
a single instance,

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:52.820
the solution is to run
on multiple instances

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:53.840
within the zone.

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:57.480
Now if any one instance fails,
the rest can pick up the load.

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:59.849
We have an HTTP or
SSL load balancer

00:18:59.849 --> 00:19:01.640
that you can put in
front of your instances

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:03.890
to spread the traffic
across those instances that

00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:05.960
will detect the failure
and direct traffic

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:08.030
to the healthy instances.

00:19:08.030 --> 00:19:12.260
You can use open source
frameworks like Kubernetes,

00:19:12.260 --> 00:19:16.400
open source databases,
for example HBase,

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:20.240
to spread your data and
your stateless processing

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:23.120
across an entire zone.

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:26.330
So we also have
hosted products which

00:19:26.330 --> 00:19:29.720
do the same thing for you but
with less overhead for you.

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:32.320
Container Engine is a
hosted Kubernetes solution.

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:35.970
Cloud Bigtable is
the same Bigtable

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:37.840
we wrote the paper
about ages ago.

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.910
It's available to you
as a hosted service.

00:19:40.910 --> 00:19:44.780
Cloud Dataflow lets you run
your Apache beam workloads

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:46.310
across an entire--
in a distributed

00:19:46.310 --> 00:19:48.860
fashion across an entire zone.

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:51.320
And Cloud Dataproc
does the same thing

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:55.424
for Hadoop and Spark workloads.

00:19:55.424 --> 00:19:56.590
So great, we're done, right?

00:19:56.590 --> 00:20:00.040
We're running across a
bunch of machines in a zone.

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:02.290
All of those bad things I
talked about that can happen

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:04.960
to your instances, you're fine.

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:06.864
Well, not quite.

00:20:06.864 --> 00:20:09.280
There are still things that
are shared by all the machines

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:10.840
running in a cluster or zone.

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:13.070
For example, they all
share that Jupiter network

00:20:13.070 --> 00:20:15.010
I was talking about.

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:17.140
They at some level
share different pieces

00:20:17.140 --> 00:20:20.410
of the power hierarchy which
feeds power to all this.

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:23.297
And they're all in the
same physical building.

00:20:23.297 --> 00:20:24.880
So if there's a fire
in that building,

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:29.260
it's going to affect more than
just one instance at a time.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:32.410
On the software side, the
cluster level operating system

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:35.140
which we've published some
information about called Borg

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:37.900
or the cluster level distributed
file system, which we've also

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:41.470
published some information
about called Colossus,

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:42.980
they can have--

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:45.310
if they get unhealthy
at a cluster level,

00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:47.740
that's going to affect more
than one instance at a time.

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:50.590
So we call all of these
things correlated failures,

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:52.180
failures that affect
or problems that

00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:55.690
affect more than one instance
in a zone at the same time

00:20:55.690 --> 00:20:59.440
because the failure mode is
related to the zone or cluster,

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:00.460
not to the--

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:03.380
something bad happening to
the individual instance.

00:21:03.380 --> 00:21:05.380
So how do we address this?

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:08.500
Well, enter regions.

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:13.000
So a region is what we
tend to call roughly

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:15.610
speaking a campus in Google.

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:19.390
You can think of it as a
set of buildings hosting

00:21:19.390 --> 00:21:22.647
Google clusters that are
in close physical proximity

00:21:22.647 --> 00:21:23.230
to each other.

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:26.380
This photo here is
of our data center

00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:28.840
campus in The Dalles,
Oregon, right there

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:30.730
on the Columbia River.

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:35.155
This is home to our
US West One region.

00:21:35.155 --> 00:21:37.030
So you can see a couple
of buildings in here.

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:39.940
Regions share less
things than zones.

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:42.190
They are on different
networking fabrics.

00:21:42.190 --> 00:21:46.570
They're running independent
Borg cluster operating system

00:21:46.570 --> 00:21:47.110
instances.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:51.250
They're running separate
distributed storage systems.

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:53.140
So a lot of the
failure modes I talked

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:56.020
about where you could
get a correlated failure

00:21:56.020 --> 00:21:58.880
within a zone will not
happen to a region at a time.

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:02.470
Additionally, we take pains
to manage our software

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:04.330
in such a way that
if we're going

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:08.560
to perform an upgrade, for
example to the Borg operating

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:12.250
system in one zone,
we're not simultaneously

00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:14.350
doing that in another zone.

00:22:14.350 --> 00:22:17.889
So that if there is a problem,
we have time to detect it

00:22:17.889 --> 00:22:19.930
and it's only going to
affect one zone at a time.

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:22.780
So we try as hard as
we can to restrict

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:27.280
these correlated failures
to a single zone.

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:31.810
However, physically
close is good

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:35.220
because we can
provide lots and lots

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:40.830
of low latency, high bandwidth
between the zones in a region.

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:44.280
But physically close
is also kind of bad

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:48.370
because for some extremely
rare forms of failure,

00:22:48.370 --> 00:22:50.380
they may be correlated
to an entire region.

00:22:50.380 --> 00:22:54.000
So for example,
earthquakes would kind of

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:57.870
affect a region all at
once or hurricanes might

00:22:57.870 --> 00:23:00.690
affect a region all at once.

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:02.970
And in one particular
made up case,

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:06.720
we had a region
attacked by zombies.

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:07.830
No, it's not real.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:11.280
We do this thing called DIRT.

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:12.900
You can see it on
the slide there.

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:15.150
You can go read about
this in the ACMQ.

00:23:15.150 --> 00:23:18.150
But basically, we do
these disaster readiness

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:19.960
tests all the time at Google.

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:23.460
So while we haven't actually
suffered a regional failure,

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:25.230
we practice things
like that to make

00:23:25.230 --> 00:23:29.590
sure we know we can handle it
if the situation comes along.

00:23:29.590 --> 00:23:33.930
So how do you protect against
these extremely rare but still

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:40.470
possible regional
scoped problems?

00:23:40.470 --> 00:23:42.480
Well, we have one
the one region.

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:45.030
So kind of the same solution.

00:23:45.030 --> 00:23:48.030
When the zone wasn't giving
you enough reliability

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:51.540
to meet your SLO and you stepped
up to running in a region,

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:54.360
multiple zones in a
region, if a single region

00:23:54.360 --> 00:23:57.360
isn't going to give you enough
resilience to meet your SLO,

00:23:57.360 --> 00:23:59.640
then you can step up and
run in multiple regions.

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:02.060
And again, the
HTTP load balancer

00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:06.300
is perfectly happy to balance
your traffic across instances

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:09.990
in multiple regions just like it
could do it in multiple loads.

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:14.970
And that works great for
stateless servers, request

00:24:14.970 --> 00:24:15.858
response servers.

00:24:19.530 --> 00:24:22.650
But what about your
persistence layers?

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:23.910
What about your storage?

00:24:23.910 --> 00:24:26.490
What about your messaging?

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:28.110
How do you run those
across regions?

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:32.670
Well, , it's a hard problem
but Google offers some services

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:36.750
in that persistent layer that
already run in what we call

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:37.530
multi-regions.

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:42.940
So a multi-region is generally--
we have three right now, United

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:45.240
States, Europe, and Asia.

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:48.480
These services run
in multiple campuses

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:50.800
across these broad
geographical areas

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:56.310
so they are not vulnerable
to single region failures.

00:24:56.310 --> 00:24:58.170
We currently have
Cloud Storage, which

00:24:58.170 --> 00:25:03.420
provides multi-region support,
Cloud Datastore, which also

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:06.420
provides multi-region support,
and Cloud Pub/Sub, which is

00:25:06.420 --> 00:25:10.470
a persistent messaging service.

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:12.970
So these are automatically
spread across regions.

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:16.710
So if you let Google worry
about your persistence layers

00:25:16.710 --> 00:25:20.280
using one of these
services, then all you

00:25:20.280 --> 00:25:24.336
have left to worry about
are your stateless servers,

00:25:24.336 --> 00:25:25.210
whatever they may be.

00:25:25.210 --> 00:25:26.584
And as we've talked
about before,

00:25:26.584 --> 00:25:29.310
you put those behind one
of our HTTP load balancers

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:32.559
and you can have that traffic
spread across multiple regions.

00:25:32.559 --> 00:25:34.350
I'm going to spend some
time digging deeper

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:37.390
into that particular thing.

00:25:37.390 --> 00:25:41.070
So I'm going to
start with a fairly--

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:43.590
a very oversimplified
version of this.

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:50.210
Here we have a
representation of a service.

00:25:50.210 --> 00:25:55.850
It's running across 10 instances
in two different zones in two

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:57.170
different regions.

00:25:57.170 --> 00:26:00.290
So it's now a
multi-regional service.

00:26:00.290 --> 00:26:03.330
So it's insulated from
individual instance failures,

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:06.380
it's insulated
from zone failures,

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:09.350
and it's insulated
from regional failures.

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:12.530
In this case, what the
numbers mean here is

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:16.190
each server can only handle
100 requests per second

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:17.510
before becoming overloaded.

00:26:17.510 --> 00:26:19.790
So I've colored them
yellow to represent hey,

00:26:19.790 --> 00:26:20.870
these are maxed out.

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:23.060
They're good but
they're maxed out.

00:26:23.060 --> 00:26:25.160
And then I carry that
yellow up to show hey,

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:28.340
we got 500 requests coming
into US East One, 500 coming

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:32.610
into Europe West One, and
1000 coming into the service

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:33.110
globally.

00:26:36.460 --> 00:26:42.710
So now what happens if, say,
US East One B goes down on us?

00:26:42.710 --> 00:26:44.430
We have one of these
correlated failures.

00:26:44.430 --> 00:26:45.140
Now what?

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:48.680
Well the load balancer will
detect that the instances

00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:49.620
are no longer healthy.

00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:51.036
It will stop sending
traffic there

00:26:51.036 --> 00:26:54.830
and all that traffic will be
directed at Europe West One.

00:26:54.830 --> 00:26:56.100
Well, now we're overloaded.

00:26:56.100 --> 00:27:01.010
Now we're asking each server to
provide 200 requests per second

00:27:01.010 --> 00:27:06.860
or 200% of what it's capable of
doing without being overloaded.

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:11.060
I'd like to put an aside in here
and say it's a really good idea

00:27:11.060 --> 00:27:14.960
to test how your service
behaves when it's overloaded.

00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:16.940
Because no matter
how much you try,

00:27:16.940 --> 00:27:18.494
sometimes things
are going to happen

00:27:18.494 --> 00:27:20.660
which is going to put your
service into a little bit

00:27:20.660 --> 00:27:23.390
or a lot of overload
and it's really nice

00:27:23.390 --> 00:27:26.450
if your service degrades
somewhat gracefully

00:27:26.450 --> 00:27:30.365
in those situations versus
a total global failure.

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:35.020
But how do we get out
of this bad situation?

00:27:35.020 --> 00:27:37.930
Well, we stand up
five new instances

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:40.060
in the zone that's
still good and we're

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:45.980
back to being able to serve
without being overloaded.

00:27:45.980 --> 00:27:51.400
Now depending on your SLO, you
could use humans to do this.

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:53.140
When your service
gets overloaded,

00:27:53.140 --> 00:27:55.960
you hopefully have a service
level indicator for that.

00:27:55.960 --> 00:27:59.425
It goes over a threshold,
a human gets paged.

00:27:59.425 --> 00:28:01.300
Five minutes later,
they're at their keyboard

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:03.130
saying, wow we're
really overloaded.

00:28:03.130 --> 00:28:05.020
I'm going to spin
up new instances

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:06.610
to take up this slack.

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:08.110
You could do it
that way but you can

00:28:08.110 --> 00:28:12.212
see that putting a human in the
loop there adds a lot of time.

00:28:12.212 --> 00:28:13.420
You're overloaded for longer.

00:28:13.420 --> 00:28:15.710
Now maybe that's
fine for your SLO,

00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:17.710
depending on how many
nines you're shooting for.

00:28:17.710 --> 00:28:20.560
If it's not, you can use
robots to do this for you.

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:23.200
Specifically, we have
something called an autoscaler.

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:26.740
And you can use that
with Container Engine,

00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:29.440
you can use that with something
called Managed Instance

00:28:29.440 --> 00:28:33.010
Groups where you
essentially tell it, hey,

00:28:33.010 --> 00:28:36.380
each instance can take
100 requests per second.

00:28:36.380 --> 00:28:38.920
So when you see
1,000 coming in here,

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:42.500
please spin up to 10 instances.

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:46.300
So this solution has-- even
this over simplified version

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:48.260
of the solution still
has two problems.

00:28:48.260 --> 00:28:51.520
The first problem is
you are putting yourself

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:53.440
in a situation where
when there is a failure,

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:56.290
you will have
temporary overload.

00:28:56.290 --> 00:28:57.220
So that's problem one.

00:28:57.220 --> 00:29:01.810
Problem two is what if you can't
spin up five more instances

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:02.920
in that replacement zone?

00:29:02.920 --> 00:29:03.420
What?

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:06.080
If you submit those
requests and it says sorry,

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:08.470
we couldn't bring up
instances for you.

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:12.220
And now you're back in overload
and you're kind of stuck there.

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:13.880
So why would this happen?

00:29:13.880 --> 00:29:18.610
Well, this is where I have
to talk about stockouts.

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:22.630
I know it may appear that
we have infinite capacity

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:26.800
but the truth of the
matter is we don't.

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:32.200
And to explain that, I want to
talk about banks for a minute.

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:34.480
So banks maintain this
wonderful illusion

00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:37.810
that I can walk up at any
time and say give me my money.

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:40.942
And as long as everybody who
is a customer of that bank

00:29:40.942 --> 00:29:42.400
or a significant
percentage of them

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:44.200
doesn't walk up at
exactly the same time

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:46.740
and ask for most or all
of their money, it's fine.

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:48.490
Because they keep a
certain amount of cash

00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:51.430
in reserve so that when
you walk up to the bank,

00:29:51.430 --> 00:29:53.080
they're able to
give you your cash.

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:56.630
So they keep this
reserve around.

00:29:56.630 --> 00:29:58.750
So Cloud capacity is
kind of like that.

00:29:58.750 --> 00:30:01.355
We keep an inventory around
such that most of the time

00:30:01.355 --> 00:30:03.730
if you come up to us and say,
hey, I need five instances,

00:30:03.730 --> 00:30:05.110
we're like great, here you go.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:06.490
Here's five instances.

00:30:09.765 --> 00:30:13.540
And as long as the
requests-- we add customers

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:16.510
at sort of a normal
rate and those customers

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:19.750
grow their services at
sort of a normal rate,

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:20.971
we can keep ahead of that.

00:30:20.971 --> 00:30:22.720
We're really good at
keeping ahead of that

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:24.720
and providing this illusion
of infinite capacity

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:26.890
so that every time you ask
for five more instances,

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:30.260
we're like, fantastic,
here you go.

00:30:30.260 --> 00:30:33.310
However, if we get a very,
very large inorganic,

00:30:33.310 --> 00:30:37.901
unpredicted spike, we could
consume our inventory.

00:30:37.901 --> 00:30:40.150
Now we're going to add
inventory as quickly as we can.

00:30:40.150 --> 00:30:42.549
It's not like a bank run
where a bank actually fails

00:30:42.549 --> 00:30:44.090
and then everybody
loses their money.

00:30:44.090 --> 00:30:49.510
We're just going to start adding
inventory as quickly as we can.

00:30:49.510 --> 00:30:51.310
But this inorganic
demand spike may

00:30:51.310 --> 00:30:55.630
cause this temporary
stockout situation.

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:58.870
What could cause such
an inorganic spike

00:30:58.870 --> 00:31:01.210
in demand in a particular cell?

00:31:01.210 --> 00:31:03.520
What if we had a cell
failure and everybody

00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:05.770
is fleeing from the
cell that's no longer

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:08.320
there into other cells?

00:31:08.320 --> 00:31:11.920
That kind of sounds like an
unexpected inorganic spike

00:31:11.920 --> 00:31:13.060
in traffic.

00:31:13.060 --> 00:31:14.950
Everybody suddenly
wants more capacity,

00:31:14.950 --> 00:31:18.040
or some fraction of our
customers wants more capacity.

00:31:18.040 --> 00:31:20.440
So how do you protect
yourself against this?

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:24.879
Well the simple solution is ask
for the instances in advance.

00:31:24.879 --> 00:31:26.920
So in this particular
case, what I'm showing here

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:30.070
is we have five instances
in each zone which

00:31:30.070 --> 00:31:34.270
are serving the traffic and
we have five spare instances

00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:35.140
in each zone.

00:31:35.140 --> 00:31:37.926
Now, this is grossly
oversimplified

00:31:37.926 --> 00:31:40.300
because load balancers are
really good, not surprisingly,

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:42.180
at balancing loads
so really, you

00:31:42.180 --> 00:31:44.110
get this where all
the instances are

00:31:44.110 --> 00:31:47.620
at 50% capacity instead
of some maxed out

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:49.240
and some doing nothing.

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:52.720
Now when the zone fails,
the traffic just spills over

00:31:52.720 --> 00:31:54.940
and now we're at 100%
capacity in the zone that's

00:31:54.940 --> 00:31:55.780
already there.

00:31:55.780 --> 00:31:58.660
You are no longer exposed
to a stockout risk

00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:02.410
because you already have
the instances you need.

00:32:02.410 --> 00:32:04.870
And you don't get a temporary
overload because you already

00:32:04.870 --> 00:32:06.700
had the capacity spun up.

00:32:06.700 --> 00:32:09.100
However, it costs you double.

00:32:09.100 --> 00:32:11.800
You're running 20 instances
now instead of the 10

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:15.940
that's required to
serve the traffic.

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:18.730
So can we do better than that?

00:32:18.730 --> 00:32:21.220
What if we go across five zones?

00:32:21.220 --> 00:32:24.970
So here, each zone is
responsible for dealing

00:32:24.970 --> 00:32:31.720
with 200 requests per second and
we have three spare instances

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:32.800
in three of the zones.

00:32:32.800 --> 00:32:37.830
Again, because load balancing,
it would really look like this.

00:32:37.830 --> 00:32:41.080
The question is, why do we have
three spares instead of two?

00:32:41.080 --> 00:32:42.650
We only need to
be able to soak up

00:32:42.650 --> 00:32:47.170
200 extra requests per
second if a zone fails.

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:49.720
And the answer is because
the zone that fails

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:52.420
could be one that
has a spare in it.

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:56.752
So you have to be prepared
for any of your zones to fail.

00:32:56.752 --> 00:32:57.460
So this is great.

00:32:57.460 --> 00:33:00.460
Again, no overload,
no stockout risk,

00:33:00.460 --> 00:33:02.176
but its only 30% overhead.

00:33:02.176 --> 00:33:03.300
This is a huge improvement.

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:06.790
We've gone from 100%
overhead to 30% overhead.

00:33:06.790 --> 00:33:08.890
Can we do even better?

00:33:08.890 --> 00:33:11.960
If five zones was
good, why not 10?

00:33:11.960 --> 00:33:14.340
So here, we only
have two spares.

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:16.340
Again, the spares
aren't really spares.

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:20.120
It would spread the
traffic out like this.

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:26.200
And now a failure of one
zone again just spills over.

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:27.070
This is great now.

00:33:27.070 --> 00:33:31.470
We're at 20% overhead and
any one zone can fail.

00:33:31.470 --> 00:33:35.590
But-- oh, this is what
we call n plus one.

00:33:35.590 --> 00:33:37.450
Any one zone can
fail and we're fine.

00:33:37.450 --> 00:33:43.060
So we have n plus
one reliability here.

00:33:43.060 --> 00:33:45.520
But we've now opened ourselves
to a new failure mode.

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:47.030
Do you see it?

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:49.120
Let me give you a hint.

00:33:49.120 --> 00:33:50.410
Hello again, Matthew.

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:53.040
So this is Hurricane Matthew.

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:57.310
Hurricane Matthew paid a visit
to South Carolina last October.

00:33:57.310 --> 00:34:00.520
That also happens to be
where US East One is.

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:03.310
In the end we ran some
generators for a while

00:34:03.310 --> 00:34:06.190
but there was no interruption to
service to any of our services.

00:34:06.190 --> 00:34:07.940
But it's an interesting
thought experiment

00:34:07.940 --> 00:34:11.920
to ask what if we had a
complete regional failure?

00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:13.239
Are you insulated against that?

00:34:13.239 --> 00:34:15.639
And in this case, that
takes out two zones.

00:34:15.639 --> 00:34:17.830
So by adding a second
zone in each region,

00:34:17.830 --> 00:34:21.190
we actually increased
our exposure

00:34:21.190 --> 00:34:22.540
to the regional outage.

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:25.989
Because before, losing a
region or losing a zone

00:34:25.989 --> 00:34:27.460
was the same thing.

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:30.530
Now they're different.

00:34:30.530 --> 00:34:33.699
So in this case, we're
back in the same position

00:34:33.699 --> 00:34:36.340
we were in before where
we're temporarily overloaded.

00:34:36.340 --> 00:34:39.380
But you'll notice we're
like 10% to 12% overloaded.

00:34:39.380 --> 00:34:42.670
It's not quite as catastrophic
a situation as it was before.

00:34:42.670 --> 00:34:46.090
And we have eight places where
if we can bring up just one

00:34:46.090 --> 00:34:49.540
instance in any of these eight
places, we're good again.

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:52.480
So the risk is a lot lower than
it was before when we were only

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:54.070
running in two zones.

00:34:54.070 --> 00:34:57.820
But if that risk is not
acceptable given your SLO,

00:34:57.820 --> 00:35:00.640
then we get back to a
situation where you're

00:35:00.640 --> 00:35:02.140
going to run three spares.

00:35:02.140 --> 00:35:06.350
So now you're at
30% overhead again,

00:35:06.350 --> 00:35:09.190
and it kind of looks a lot
like the five zone solution.

00:35:09.190 --> 00:35:13.470
But now we're almost at
what we would call n plus 2

00:35:13.470 --> 00:35:17.500
where I can almost lose any
two zones and I'm still good.

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:19.120
It's only if I lose
two of the zones

00:35:19.120 --> 00:35:24.070
with the spares in it
where I'm at a problem.

00:35:24.070 --> 00:35:26.560
So running in 10
zones is better than

00:35:26.560 --> 00:35:31.510
running in five because you
almost get to n plus 2 with 30%

00:35:31.510 --> 00:35:32.590
overhead whereas before.

00:35:32.590 --> 00:35:35.300
You were only at n plus
one with 30% overhead.

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:37.155
So there has been
an improvement.

00:35:39.910 --> 00:35:42.250
So one of the simplifying
things-- assumptions I've made

00:35:42.250 --> 00:35:45.010
is this notion that
we have exactly 1,000

00:35:45.010 --> 00:35:47.950
requests per second flowing
in all day, every day.

00:35:47.950 --> 00:35:51.160
Every day is the same and
we can statically choose

00:35:51.160 --> 00:35:52.420
how many servers we have.

00:35:52.420 --> 00:35:55.510
Of course that's not
how most services are.

00:35:55.510 --> 00:35:57.910
Let's pretend we
have a service that

00:35:57.910 --> 00:36:02.350
has [INAUDIBLE] cycle where
1,000 is the trough and 2,000

00:36:02.350 --> 00:36:03.520
is the peak.

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:05.710
So let's look at what it
looks like at the peak.

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:09.130
And let's expand our
footprint into Asia Northeast.

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:12.880
Now we're running in 12 zones.

00:36:12.880 --> 00:36:15.760
And let's make all
the zones the same.

00:36:15.760 --> 00:36:19.390
If we run three
instances per region,

00:36:19.390 --> 00:36:20.950
we don't quite have
enough capacity

00:36:20.950 --> 00:36:22.840
and if we run four
instances per region,

00:36:22.840 --> 00:36:25.614
it looks like we're
wasting capacity

00:36:25.614 --> 00:36:27.780
because all these things
are running in the mid 80s.

00:36:31.630 --> 00:36:33.640
And the overhead
here is about 20%.

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:37.570
We're running 24
instances when 20

00:36:37.570 --> 00:36:40.000
is all that should
be required to serve

00:36:40.000 --> 00:36:42.150
2000 requests per second.

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:43.930
That 20% overhead kind
of sounds familiar.

00:36:43.930 --> 00:36:46.590
That's where we ended up before.

00:36:46.590 --> 00:36:50.920
Now if we lose a single
zone, life's not too bad.

00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:52.900
A bunch of it can failover
within the region,

00:36:52.900 --> 00:36:53.600
which is good.

00:36:53.600 --> 00:36:55.510
And then the rest can
sort of spill over

00:36:55.510 --> 00:36:59.770
to the other regions which
are not running super hot.

00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:02.620
And if we lose two zones,
whether it's in a region

00:37:02.620 --> 00:37:04.755
or across two
regions, it doesn't

00:37:04.755 --> 00:37:06.880
matter because all the
zones are now the same size.

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:08.640
That's kind of cool.

00:37:08.640 --> 00:37:13.780
And we can lose two zones and
we are now running at 100%.

00:37:13.780 --> 00:37:16.870
No overload, no stockout risk.

00:37:16.870 --> 00:37:18.380
That was for the peak of 2000.

00:37:18.380 --> 00:37:22.420
So basically with 20% overhead
running across 12 zones,

00:37:22.420 --> 00:37:25.567
we've now got to n plus
two, a true n plus two.

00:37:25.567 --> 00:37:26.650
We can lose any two zones.

00:37:29.200 --> 00:37:31.110
So that was the peak.

00:37:31.110 --> 00:37:35.280
But for the trough, if we
stick with our static two

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:39.420
instances per zone, now we're
vastly over provisioned.

00:37:39.420 --> 00:37:40.920
Like, we're running
down in the 40%.

00:37:40.920 --> 00:37:42.830
This is a huge waste of--

00:37:42.830 --> 00:37:44.220
is not a reliability problem.

00:37:44.220 --> 00:37:47.100
Clearly we can withstand all
kinds of failures right now.

00:37:47.100 --> 00:37:49.747
But it is a cost problem.

00:37:49.747 --> 00:37:51.330
If only there was
something that would

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:55.200
scale the number of servers
automatically to the amount

00:37:55.200 --> 00:37:56.552
of load that was coming in.

00:37:56.552 --> 00:37:58.510
But of course there is,
I mentioned it earlier.

00:37:58.510 --> 00:38:01.080
We can use managed instance
groups or our Container engine

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:02.380
to do that for us.

00:38:02.380 --> 00:38:05.550
So what if it's scaled
down to one instance?

00:38:05.550 --> 00:38:07.500
Now we're in this
situation where

00:38:07.500 --> 00:38:12.960
we have one instance per zone
and we're still at n plus two.

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:16.080
So life looks pretty good.

00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:18.660
So the autoscaler
can save you money

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:24.030
by reducing your number of
instances during your troughs.

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:26.220
And if you configure
it with some slacks,

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:29.730
so you sort of say, hey I want
to run around 80% utilization,

00:38:29.730 --> 00:38:33.240
then if you suffer a
failure, that 20% becomes

00:38:33.240 --> 00:38:39.040
where that traffic goes
in the case of a failure.

00:38:39.040 --> 00:38:42.600
So now we're back to our 2000.

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:43.931
Life is good again.

00:38:43.931 --> 00:38:45.930
The other simplifying
assumption I've made here,

00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:48.600
you'll notice that our
traffic is beautifully

00:38:48.600 --> 00:38:52.620
spread absolutely evenly
throughout all of our regions.

00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:55.080
If only the world
could do this for us.

00:38:55.080 --> 00:38:56.520
It's never going
to be like that.

00:38:56.520 --> 00:38:57.978
Your traffic comes
from everywhere.

00:38:57.978 --> 00:39:01.540
You don't get to pick where
your traffic comes from.

00:39:01.540 --> 00:39:08.010
So for most-- for many services,
the offered traffic kind of

00:39:08.010 --> 00:39:10.140
follows the sun
because it's based

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:13.470
on humans being awake and
doing things with computers.

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:15.554
And you want to serve
as close as you can

00:39:15.554 --> 00:39:17.595
to the people who are
submitting requests to you.

00:39:17.595 --> 00:39:19.345
You don't want to send
the request halfway

00:39:19.345 --> 00:39:22.470
around the world if
you can avoid it.

00:39:22.470 --> 00:39:24.150
So a fairly typical
service might

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:28.440
have a peak around 1700 UTC.

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:31.957
That's the evening
in Europe and it's

00:39:31.957 --> 00:39:33.540
the morning on the
West Coast and it's

00:39:33.540 --> 00:39:36.312
the middle of the day on the
east coast of North America.

00:39:36.312 --> 00:39:38.520
So that's when some services
sort of have their peak.

00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:40.620
So let's pretend
that we're doing that

00:39:40.620 --> 00:39:43.110
and we have kind
of this pattern.

00:39:43.110 --> 00:39:50.100
With our static two servers, two
instances per zone, what we see

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:52.980
is we're offering a lot
more traffic in Europe

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:56.430
than it can handle so it kind
of spills over to US East which

00:39:56.430 --> 00:39:58.710
is now spilling over to
US Central which is now

00:39:58.710 --> 00:40:01.020
spilling over to US West One.

00:40:01.020 --> 00:40:01.980
It works.

00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:03.070
We're not overloaded.

00:40:03.070 --> 00:40:05.160
All the requests get served.

00:40:05.160 --> 00:40:07.725
But we're not serving
them as close to our users

00:40:07.725 --> 00:40:09.600
as we might like which
means they're probably

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:11.580
seeing additional latency.

00:40:11.580 --> 00:40:15.990
And if we lose Europe West One,
it's going to get kind of ugly

00:40:15.990 --> 00:40:16.740
at that point.

00:40:16.740 --> 00:40:19.620
We're going to be
doing a lot spilling.

00:40:19.620 --> 00:40:22.670
So instead, what if we use
our autoscaler and we say,

00:40:22.670 --> 00:40:27.820
hey, run as many services as
you need to keep under 80%.

00:40:27.820 --> 00:40:30.370
You're going to end up with
something that looks like this.

00:40:30.370 --> 00:40:33.080
And there's some overhead here.

00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:39.990
We're running 27 instances total
when you should only need 20.

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:42.360
But it's all in--
it's all region, which

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:45.530
means you're giving your
users a better experience.

00:40:45.530 --> 00:40:48.870
You're serving them
closer to where they live.

00:40:48.870 --> 00:40:51.690
And you've got a lot of spare
capacity kicking around here.

00:40:51.690 --> 00:40:57.180
So if you lose one of your
big zones, a lot of that slack

00:40:57.180 --> 00:40:58.980
can be taken up
in region and then

00:40:58.980 --> 00:41:01.470
there's a little bit of
spill over to another region.

00:41:01.470 --> 00:41:04.080
But of course you have an
autoscaler on these things.

00:41:04.080 --> 00:41:09.870
So those instances that are
now running at higher than 80%

00:41:09.870 --> 00:41:11.670
are going to feed back
to the autoscaler.

00:41:11.670 --> 00:41:14.622
Hey by the way, I'm above
80% and the autoscaler

00:41:14.622 --> 00:41:16.080
is going to say,
well gee, I better

00:41:16.080 --> 00:41:18.627
bring up some more capacity.

00:41:18.627 --> 00:41:20.460
In the end, you should
converge to something

00:41:20.460 --> 00:41:24.300
like this where the traffic
is being offered in region

00:41:24.300 --> 00:41:27.570
and the four instances we
lost from the failed zone

00:41:27.570 --> 00:41:30.630
get brought up automatically
in the same-- in another zone

00:41:30.630 --> 00:41:31.470
in the same region.

00:41:34.779 --> 00:41:36.320
Wouldn't it be nice
if we didn't have

00:41:36.320 --> 00:41:37.850
to worry about zones though?

00:41:37.850 --> 00:41:39.182
We thought so too.

00:41:39.182 --> 00:41:41.390
So if you like the autoscaler
and you like that idea,

00:41:41.390 --> 00:41:43.681
we've got this thing called
a Regional Managed Instance

00:41:43.681 --> 00:41:44.480
Group.

00:41:44.480 --> 00:41:46.940
And basically what
it says is, hey,

00:41:46.940 --> 00:41:49.910
run me some instances in
these zones in these regions

00:41:49.910 --> 00:41:52.550
and I don't care that much
about what zones they're in.

00:41:52.550 --> 00:41:54.900
Just make sure I've got
enough in each region.

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:57.350
So it takes us from
here where we're

00:41:57.350 --> 00:42:00.650
caring about what zone things
are in to this mental model

00:42:00.650 --> 00:42:03.480
where we're only caring
about what regions are in.

00:42:07.890 --> 00:42:12.155
So, I talked a lot
about the autoscaler.

00:42:12.155 --> 00:42:14.280
If you want to know more
about that, if that sounds

00:42:14.280 --> 00:42:15.870
like something you
could use, there's

00:42:15.870 --> 00:42:18.810
going to be a talk tomorrow.

00:42:18.810 --> 00:42:21.120
I encourage you to go see it
if that sounds like a tool

00:42:21.120 --> 00:42:22.953
that you would like to
have in your toolbox.

00:42:25.940 --> 00:42:28.580
So that was a lot of
slides on traffic spilling

00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:30.425
here and traffic spilling
there and scaling

00:42:30.425 --> 00:42:32.300
automatically and where
do you want to run it

00:42:32.300 --> 00:42:34.820
and things like that.

00:42:34.820 --> 00:42:36.920
There's a different path
if you want to take it

00:42:36.920 --> 00:42:39.470
and that is App Engine.

00:42:39.470 --> 00:42:43.160
App Engine is sort of our--

00:42:43.160 --> 00:42:45.626
it has all of this
built in, so to speak.

00:42:45.626 --> 00:42:47.000
You just give us
your code and we

00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:53.260
run it and you get auto scaling
and you get the failover.

00:42:53.260 --> 00:42:55.370
We're handling all these
things under the cover.

00:42:55.370 --> 00:42:59.490
You give us your code and
your application runs.

00:42:59.490 --> 00:43:01.310
So if that sounds
interesting to you,

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:03.860
I encourage you to check
out this talk which

00:43:03.860 --> 00:43:07.670
is Writing Infinitely Scalable
and High Performance Apps

00:43:07.670 --> 00:43:09.410
with App Engine.

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:11.715
And if you might be sitting
in the audience saying,

00:43:11.715 --> 00:43:14.090
you know what, I checked that
out two to three years ago.

00:43:14.090 --> 00:43:16.340
It didn't fit for
what I wanted to do.

00:43:19.091 --> 00:43:21.090
I encourage you to check
out this talk which is,

00:43:21.090 --> 00:43:23.340
You Can Run That on App Engine?

00:43:23.340 --> 00:43:25.650
We've done a lot to
the service recently.

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:27.582
And if you have looked
at it in the past

00:43:27.582 --> 00:43:29.040
but have not looked
at it recently,

00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:32.460
you might find that it
fits what you need to do

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:34.090
a lot better than it used to.

00:43:34.090 --> 00:43:36.300
So I'm not going to go
any more in-depth on that.

00:43:36.300 --> 00:43:37.815
Go see these experts.

00:43:40.787 --> 00:43:42.620
So a quick recap, what
have we talked about?

00:43:42.620 --> 00:43:44.162
We talked about
reliability and trust

00:43:44.162 --> 00:43:45.744
and how they're
related to each other.

00:43:45.744 --> 00:43:48.150
We talked about service level
indicators, service level

00:43:48.150 --> 00:43:52.050
objectives, service level
agreements, and error budgets.

00:43:52.050 --> 00:43:53.940
We've gone through
instances, zones, regions,

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:55.230
and multi-regions.

00:43:55.230 --> 00:43:58.230
We've talked about the bad
things that can happen to them.

00:43:58.230 --> 00:43:59.790
And then we've talked
in great detail

00:43:59.790 --> 00:44:02.670
about how to spread across
these zones and regions

00:44:02.670 --> 00:44:06.420
and how to avoid
overload and stockouts.

00:44:06.420 --> 00:44:10.050
So in a sense, what I've
done is I have exposed you

00:44:10.050 --> 00:44:13.110
to what I think are some
really awesome tools

00:44:13.110 --> 00:44:15.540
for building super
reliable systems.

00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:19.520
Like, if you have
dig a hole, that's

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:22.310
going to dig you a
really good hole.

00:44:22.310 --> 00:44:23.630
I'd like to use that.

00:44:23.630 --> 00:44:25.280
That looks like fun.

00:44:25.280 --> 00:44:30.970
But really sometimes, you
don't need a hole that big.

00:44:30.970 --> 00:44:34.660
Sometimes a shovel will
do the job just fine.

00:44:34.660 --> 00:44:36.600
So again, how do
you decide if you

00:44:36.600 --> 00:44:39.870
want to use the
shovel or the backhoe?

00:44:39.870 --> 00:44:42.680
You look at your
service level objective.

00:44:42.680 --> 00:44:45.080
So here are my
rough rules of thumb

00:44:45.080 --> 00:44:46.490
about when you need to go--

00:44:46.490 --> 00:44:52.520
the backhoe is on the far right,
the shovel is on the far left.

00:44:52.520 --> 00:44:56.270
If you're looking for less
than two nines of reliability,

00:44:56.270 --> 00:44:58.760
you're probably going to be
just fine on a single instance.

00:44:58.760 --> 00:45:00.260
You've got enough
error budget there

00:45:00.260 --> 00:45:02.230
that if your instance
fails, you just--

00:45:02.230 --> 00:45:06.800
you take your backups
which you have, right?

00:45:06.800 --> 00:45:11.600
And you restore them on another
instance and off you go.

00:45:11.600 --> 00:45:13.856
Or if everything is
already in persistent disk

00:45:13.856 --> 00:45:15.230
and you've been
taking snapshots,

00:45:15.230 --> 00:45:17.610
then it just comes
back up for you.

00:45:17.610 --> 00:45:20.570
So that might be totally fine.

00:45:20.570 --> 00:45:21.470
A zone fails.

00:45:21.470 --> 00:45:24.470
Well, you might have to do some
manual stuff where you bring it

00:45:24.470 --> 00:45:26.570
up in another zone, but
you've got enough time

00:45:26.570 --> 00:45:30.230
in your error budget for
humans to take these actions.

00:45:30.230 --> 00:45:32.960
So you don't need the
backhoe for that job.

00:45:32.960 --> 00:45:35.780
If you want a couple of nines,
running in a single zone

00:45:35.780 --> 00:45:37.562
should be perfectly adequate.

00:45:37.562 --> 00:45:39.020
When you get up to
three nines, you

00:45:39.020 --> 00:45:41.090
should be looking at
running in a single region.

00:45:41.090 --> 00:45:42.710
If you want four
nines, now you need

00:45:42.710 --> 00:45:44.450
to be spread across
multiple regions

00:45:44.450 --> 00:45:46.350
if you really want
to achieve that.

00:45:46.350 --> 00:45:50.314
Now, before I get to
the five nines case,

00:45:50.314 --> 00:45:52.230
there's a very important
point I want to make.

00:45:52.230 --> 00:45:54.870
This is sort of
the price of entry.

00:45:54.870 --> 00:45:58.650
This is-- if you build on the
single zone, single region,

00:45:58.650 --> 00:46:01.350
multiple regions,
you have the ability

00:46:01.350 --> 00:46:03.600
to get two nines or three
nines or four nines.

00:46:03.600 --> 00:46:06.210
But it's not a
guarantee depending

00:46:06.210 --> 00:46:07.720
on what you put on top of it.

00:46:07.720 --> 00:46:12.510
You need to manage your stuff
with reliability as well.

00:46:12.510 --> 00:46:14.940
The number of nines
your customer sees

00:46:14.940 --> 00:46:16.770
depends not just on
our infrastructure,

00:46:16.770 --> 00:46:19.200
but also on your
part of the stack.

00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:22.320
So this is guidance but it
doesn't mean you will get this

00:46:22.320 --> 00:46:23.460
if you follow it.

00:46:23.460 --> 00:46:25.230
Everything needs
to work together

00:46:25.230 --> 00:46:26.970
to provide those nines
to your customers.

00:46:26.970 --> 00:46:29.215
Which brings me to
the five nines case.

00:46:29.215 --> 00:46:30.590
If you're looking
for five nines,

00:46:30.590 --> 00:46:32.880
you should probably
be talking to CRE.

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:35.160
What's CRE?

00:46:35.160 --> 00:46:39.030
CRE is Customer
Reliability Engineering.

00:46:39.030 --> 00:46:41.250
So, it's what you get when
you take the principles

00:46:41.250 --> 00:46:46.540
and lessons of SRE and apply
them towards customers.

00:46:46.540 --> 00:46:50.970
It means Google SREs working
with your SRE or ops team

00:46:50.970 --> 00:46:53.377
to achieve your desired
reliability goals.

00:46:53.377 --> 00:46:54.960
If that sounds like
I'm quoting, I am.

00:46:57.510 --> 00:47:02.550
we want to drive more nines
to our mutual customers.

00:47:02.550 --> 00:47:04.230
You're running your
stuff on our stuff.

00:47:04.230 --> 00:47:06.660
We want our mutual
customers to be happy.

00:47:06.660 --> 00:47:10.410
Customer Reliability
Engineering is an effort

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:11.649
to make that happen.

00:47:11.649 --> 00:47:13.065
If this sounds
interesting to you,

00:47:13.065 --> 00:47:14.730
you should talk
to your sales rep

00:47:14.730 --> 00:47:18.180
and they can start
that conversation.

00:47:18.180 --> 00:47:22.110
Now if you'd like to learn
more about Customer Reliability

00:47:22.110 --> 00:47:24.810
Engineering, there's
a talk for that.

00:47:24.810 --> 00:47:29.240
This is a talk by Luke Stone.

00:47:29.240 --> 00:47:32.400
Luke, is that you out there?

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:33.370
No, all right.

00:47:33.370 --> 00:47:35.920
I thought Luke was
in the audience.

00:47:35.920 --> 00:47:39.470
This is a talk by Luke Stone,
in fact, right after this.

00:47:39.470 --> 00:47:41.650
Please add 30 minutes
to all of these times

00:47:41.650 --> 00:47:45.670
because I didn't know the
keynote was going to run over.

00:47:45.670 --> 00:47:47.740
So if you are interested
in learning more

00:47:47.740 --> 00:47:50.890
about what a CRE
engagement looks like,

00:47:50.890 --> 00:47:52.390
this is the talk for you.

00:47:52.390 --> 00:47:56.410
And then Luke also has some war
stories to share from his time

00:47:56.410 --> 00:47:59.874
as an SRE and as a director
of this CRE initiative.

00:47:59.874 --> 00:48:01.540
So I encourage you
to go check those out

00:48:01.540 --> 00:48:03.814
if this is interesting to you.

00:48:03.814 --> 00:48:05.230
The last thing
I'll leave you with

00:48:05.230 --> 00:48:07.660
is come drop by the
fifth nine lounge.

00:48:07.660 --> 00:48:09.730
If you are interested
in reliability,

00:48:09.730 --> 00:48:13.390
if you're the sort of person
who carries quote a pager,

00:48:13.390 --> 00:48:14.320
come hang out.

00:48:14.320 --> 00:48:17.920
Your tribe is there at the
fifth nine on the third floor.

00:48:17.920 --> 00:48:21.320
There will be a book signing
as I mentioned earlier.

00:48:21.320 --> 00:48:25.750
And there's lots of
Google SREs and CREs there

00:48:25.750 --> 00:48:27.160
for you to chat with.

00:48:27.160 --> 00:48:28.750
I plan to spend a
lot of time there so

00:48:28.750 --> 00:48:31.540
if you think of something
you want to talk to me about

00:48:31.540 --> 00:48:34.420
or you just want to tell
me where my talk was wrong,

00:48:34.420 --> 00:48:34.990
come on up.

00:48:34.990 --> 00:48:38.000
I'd love to hear it.

00:48:38.000 --> 00:48:40.209
Thank you so much for
giving me your time today.

00:48:40.209 --> 00:48:41.500
I really, really appreciate it.

00:48:41.500 --> 00:48:44.460
I sincerely hope you
found this worthwhile.

00:48:44.460 --> 00:48:49.310
